## Why This Is Asked
Analyzes streaming-frequency estimation with bounded memory and batch processing.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy hitters; counter trimming; candidate set.
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs memory; exact counting as baseline.
- Batch processing implications on update costs.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Misra-Gries skeleton
class MG {
  constructor(K){ this.K=K; this.map=new Map(); }
  add(x){ if(this.map.has(x)) this.map.set(x,this.map.get(x)+1); else if(this.map.size<this.K) this.map.set(x,1); else { for(let [k,c] of this.map){ this.map.set(k,c-1); } /* remove zeros */ for(let [k,c] of Array.from(this.map)) if(this.map.get(k)<=0) this.map.delete(k); } }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of streaming distinct-count with memory constraints and practical trade-offs between accuracy and memory.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog sketch
- register count m controls error
- per-element O(1) update, O(m) memory, mergeable sketches

## Code Example
```javascript
class HyperLogLog {
  constructor(m = 16) {
    this.m = m; this.registers = new Uint8Array(m);
  }
  hash(x) {
    let h = 2166136261;
    for (let i = 0; i < x.length; i++) h = (h ^ x.charCodeAt(i)) * 16777619 >>> 0;
    return h;
  }
  add(x) {
    const h = this.hash(x);
    const idx = h & (this.m - 1);
    const w = h >>> Math.log2(this.m);
    const leading = Math.clz32(w) + 1;
    this.registers[idx] = Math.max(this.registers[idx], leading);
  }
  estimate() {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const r of this.registers) sum += Math.pow(2, -r);
    const alpha = 0.7213 / (1 + 1.079 / this.m);
    return alpha * this.m * this.m / sum;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does choosing m affect relative error and memory? 
- How would you merge two sketches in a distributed setup? 
- What pitfalls exist with non-uniform ID distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on complexity with top-k heavy hitters in streaming data, bounded memory, and batch updates. Tests ability to reason about error guarantees and practical trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming top-k with bounded memory
- Space-Saving / Frequent Items algorithms
- Per-element vs batch cost
- Error guarantees: ε ≤ total_count/k
- Trade-offs vs exact O(M) memory

## Code Example
```javascript
class SpaceSaving {
  constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.map=new Map(); this.counterHeap=[]; }
  update(x){ if(this.map.has(x)){ this.map.get(x).cnt++; return; }
    if(this.map.size < this.k){ this.map.set(x,{cnt:1}); return; }
    // replace min counter item
    let minKey = null, minCnt = Infinity;
    for(const [key,v] of this.map){ if(v.cnt < minCnt){ minCnt=v.cnt; minKey=key; } }
    this.map.delete(minKey);
    this.map.set(x,{cnt:minCnt+1});
  }
  topK(){ // shallow: return current keys with counts
    return Array.from(this.map.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1].cnt-a[1].cnt).map(([k,v])=>({key:k,count:v.cnt}));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design streaming algorithms with bounded memory while computing exact results. It captures a common real-world problem: maintaining aggregate statistics over a sliding or growing dataset without storing all historical values.

## Key Concepts

- **Streaming data structures:** Using heaps to maintain top-K elements efficiently
- **Running sum:** Incremental updates avoid recomputing the sum after each batch
- **Time complexity:** O(log K) per element; batch processing takes O(B log K)
- **Memory usage:** O(K) vs O(N) for the exact method that stores all past values

## Code Example

```javascript
function topKSumStream(valueStream, K) {
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  let sum = 0;

  for (const batch of valueStream) {
    for (const v of batch) {
      if (heap.size() < K) {
        heap.push(v);
        sum += v;
      } else if (heap.size() > 0 && v > heap.peek()) {
        sum += v - heap.pop();
        heap.push(v);
      }
    }
    console.log("Top-K sum:", sum);
  }
}
```

## Asymptotic Analysis

| Metric | Our Solution | Exact Method |
|--------|--------------|--------------|
| Per-element update | O(log K) | O(1) |
| Batch time (size B) | O(B log K) | O(B) |
| Memory | O(K) | O(N) |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating

