## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic spatial indices under batched inserts and to reason about amortized bounds, practical thresholds, and rebuild strategies in proximity queries common in ML data pipelines or trading systems.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-grid hashing and locality
- Amortized analysis with batched updates
- Rebuild/rehash trade-offs and density handling
- Space-time trade-offs vs brute force

## Code Example
```javascript
class GridIndex {
  constructor(D){ this.D=D; this.s=D/Math.sqrt(2); this.buckets=new Map(); }
  cell(coord){ return Math.floor(coord/this.s); }
  key(x,y){ const cx = this.cell(x); const cy = this.cell(y); return cx+','+cy; }
  insert(p){ const x=p[0], y=p[1];
    for(let i=-1;i<=1;i++) for(let j=-1;j<=1;j++){
      const k=(Math.floor(x/this.s)+i)+','+(Math.floor(y/this.s)+j);
      const bucket=this.buckets.get(k); if(bucket){ for(const q of bucket){ if(Math.hypot(x-q[0],y-q[1])<=this.D) return true; } }
    }
    const kk=Math.floor(x/this.s)+','+Math.floor(y/this.s);
    if(!this.buckets.has(kk)) this.buckets.set(kk, []);
    this.buckets.get(kk).push(p);
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or moving points?
- What are worst-case scenarios if density spikes in a single bucket, and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical streaming analytics under fixed memory, focusing on real-time percentile estimation and how rebinding affects accuracy and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms, fixed memory, boundary recomputation, percentile estimation, amortized analysis.
- Trade-offs between K, R, and accuracy; cache-friendly bucket access.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Histogram85 {
  constructor(M, K) {
    this.M = M; this.K = K;
    this.bounds = Array.from({length: K}, (_,i)=> Math.floor((i+1)*M/K));
    this.counts = new Array(K).fill(0);
  }
  update(x){
    let bucket = Math.min(this.K-1, Math.floor(x/ (this.M/this.K)));
    this.counts[bucket]++;
  }
  total(){ return this.counts.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0); }
  percentile(p){ // p in [0,1]
    const total = this.total();
    const target = p * total;
    let acc = 0;
    for(let i=this.K-1; i>=0; i--){
      acc += this.counts[i];
      if(acc >= target) return this.bounds[i-1] !== undefined ? this.bounds[i-1] : this.M;
    }
    return this.M;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you choose K and R for skewed data?
- How does this scale with M and batch size B?

